# Programming Online Behavioral Experiments Using .NET Framework for Amazon Mechanical Turk

Amazon Mechanical Turk (MTurk) is a platform where you can conduct behavioral experiments with human subjects. To effectively utilize MTurk for your experiments, you need to create a web application that adheres to MTurk's External Question framework. This allows you to host your experiment online and seamlessly integrate it with MTurk.

In this blog post, we'll discuss how to adapt your experiment website to work with MTurk using the .NET Framework. We'll focus on handling the worker ID at the beginning of the experiment and sending these details back to MTurk upon completion.

## Setting Up the Experiment Website

### Step 1: Initialize the Experiment

When participants start your experiment, they will be redirected from MTurk to your custom URL. Your job is to extract the `workerId` parameter from the URL query string. This `workerId` is unique to each worker who completes your HIT (Human Intelligence Task).

Here’s how you can initialize your experiment in a .NET MVC application:

```csharp
public class ExperimentController : Controller
{
    public ActionResult Index()
    {
        // Extract the worker ID from the query string
        var workerId = Request.QueryString["WorkerId"];

        // Log the worker ID or perform any necessary initialization
        // For example:
        // Logger.Log($"Worker ID: {workerId}");

        // Redirect the user to the actual experiment page
        return RedirectToAction("ExperimentPage", new { workerId });
    }
}
```

In this code, we use `Request.QueryString["WorkerId"]` to retrieve the `workerId` from the query string. Once you have the `workerId`, you can log it or perform any necessary initialization before redirecting the user to the main experiment page.

### Step 2: Display the Experiment

Next, you need to display the actual content of your experiment. In this step, you’ll handle the logic to present the tasks and collect data from the participants.

```html
@model string
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Behavioral Experiment</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Welcome, Worker @Model!</h1>
    <p>This is where your experiment will run.</p>
    <!-- Add your experiment logic here -->
    <button id="submitBtn">Submit</button>

    <script>
        document.getElementById('submitBtn').addEventListener('click', function() {
            fetch('/Experiment/Submit', {
                method: 'POST',
                headers: {
                    'Content-Type': 'application/json'
                },
                body: JSON.stringify({ workerId: '@Model' })
            }).then(response => response.json())
              .then(data => {
                  if (data.success) {
                      console.log('Experiment submitted successfully!');
                      // Optionally, you can redirect to thank you page or next task
                  } else {
                      console.error('Error submitting experiment:', data.error);
                  }
              })
              .catch(error => console.error('Error:', error));
        });
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

In this example, we use a simple HTML structure with a button to submit the experiment. When the button is clicked, it triggers an AJAX request to the `/Experiment/Submit` endpoint. The `workerId` is sent along with the request as part of the payload.

